  <title>Global voice settings</title>
  <p>
    Use "Global Voice Settings" preferences to configure speech behavior shared by all voice
    types and voice sets.
  </p>
  <p>
    To configure the voices Orca uses, use the separate "Voice Sets" preferences subgroup. The
    "Global" voice set holds the voices Orca normally uses. You can add voice sets for other
    languages to use when switching languages.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each voice set consists of four voice types: the default voice is used for presenting
    on-screen objects and text, the uppercase voice identifies capital letters, the hyperlink
    voice identifies links, and the system voice presents information supplied by Orca, such as
    roles, states, and messages. All four initially sound the same. Any voice type you have not
    explicitly configured uses the default voice's settings. To make the uppercase, hyperlink,
    or system voice sound different, configure it in "Voice Sets" preferences.
  </p>
  <section id="settings-global-voice-settings">
    <title>Settings</title>
    <p>
      <em style="strong">Preferences location:</em>
      <gui>Speech</gui> category, <gui>Global Voice Settings</gui> group.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em style="strong">Available settings:</em>
    </p>
    <list>
      <item>
        <p>
          <gui>Global Voice Settings</gui>
        </p>
        <list>
          <item>
            <p>
              <gui>Speech system:</gui> Selects the software
              Orca uses to communicate with speech synthesizers. Supported systems include
              Speech Dispatcher and Spiel.
            </p>
          </item>
          <item>
            <p>
              <gui>Speech synthesizer:</gui> Selects the engine
              that converts text into speech. Examples include espeak-ng and Voxin. The choices
              come from the selected speech system: output modules in Speech Dispatcher or
              providers in Spiel. To install and configure additional synthesizers or voices,
              refer to your speech system's or synthesizer's documentation.
            </p>
          </item>
          <item>
            <p>
              <gui>Punctuation Level</gui>: Controls how much
              punctuation Orca speaks. Choices: None, Some, Most, All.
            </p>
            <list>
              <item>
                <p>
                  <gui>None</gui>: Requests that
                  punctuation symbols not be spoken.
                </p>
              </item>
              <item>
                <p>
                  <gui>Some</gui>: Requests that some
                  punctuation symbols be spoken. The speech system determines which symbols are
                  included.
                </p>
              </item>
              <item>
                <p>
                  <gui>Most (default)</gui>: Requests that most
                  punctuation symbols be spoken. The speech system determines which symbols are
                  included.
                </p>
              </item>
              <item>
                <p>
                  <gui>All</gui>: Requests that all
                  punctuation symbols be spoken.
                </p>
              </item>
            </list>
          </item>
          <item>
            <p>
              <gui>Capitalization style</gui>: Controls how Orca
              indicates uppercase letters. Choices: Voice only, Icon, Spell. Default: Voice
              only.
            </p>
            <list>
              <item>
                <p>
                  <gui>Voice only</gui>: Uses the
                  configured uppercase voice without adding another indication. To hear
                  uppercase letters at a higher pitch, configure the uppercase voice in "Voice
                  Sets" preferences.
                </p>
              </item>
              <item>
                <p>
                  <gui>Icon</gui>: Uses the configured
                  uppercase voice and plays a tone before speaking an uppercase letter.
                </p>
              </item>
              <item>
                <p>
                  <gui>Spell</gui>: Uses the configured
                  uppercase voice and says capital before speaking an uppercase letter.
                </p>
              </item>
            </list>
          </item>
          <item>
            <p>
              <gui>Speak numbers as digits</gui>: When enabled, Orca
              speaks 123 as 1 2 3. When disabled, Orca sends the number to the synthesizer,
              which likely speaks it as one hundred and twenty three. Default: off.
            </p>
          </item>
          <item>
            <p>
              <gui>Speak colors as names</gui>: Controls whether
              Orca speaks colors by name, such as light blue, instead of as RGB values. Default:
              on.
            </p>
          </item>
          <item>
            <p>
              <gui>Insert pauses to break up speech</gui>: Controls whether
              Orca inserts brief pauses between parts of a spoken presentation, such as name,
              role, state, mnemonic, and tutorial message. Default: on.
            </p>
          </item>
        </list>
      </item>
    </list>
  </section>
